<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="PowerPC:BE:32:default">
<!--
    kuna strict fix (no option): a two-register value on a big-endian ABI is
    joined high word first.

    The SVR4 PowerPC ABI returns a long long in r3:r4 with the HIGH word in
    r3, the first register of the pair.  MIPS o32 ($2:$3), SPARC (%o0:%o1) and
    ARM big-endian (r0:r1) do the same: a register pair holds the value as if
    it were loaded from memory, so the lower-addressed, most significant word
    lands in the first register.  The cspec output rule says so (a join rule
    consumes the most significant piece first on a big-endian target), but
    return recovery (ActionReturnRecovery::buildReturnOutput) and the
    call-output pair (FuncCallSpecs::buildOutputFromTrials) joined the first
    register as the LOW word, so every such return printed its halves
    swapped:

        wide_mul:    return CONCAT44(a1 * a0,(int4)(... >> 0x20));   (bug)
        add_one:     return CONCAT44(a1 + 1,a0 + carry);              (bug)
        triple_plus: v1 = (uint4)(v2 >> 0x20) reads r4 as the high word

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype).  No option: this only
    corrects wrong output, and every little-endian target is byte-identical.

    Source (clang 14 -O2, powerpc-linux-gnu, bytes from bejoin_ppc32_be.o):
        long long wide_mul(int a, int b) { return (long long)a * b; }
          mulhw r5,r4,r3 / mullw r4,r4,r3 / mr r3,r5 / blr
        long long add_one(long long v) { return v + 1; }
          addic r4,r4,1 / addze r3,r3 / blr          (v arrives as r3:r4)
        static long long triple(int x) { return (long long)x * 3; }
        long long triple_plus(int x) { return triple(x) + 7; }
          bl triple / addic r4,r4,7 / addze r3,r3    (a call output pair)

    Asserts #1-#3: each value is joined high word (r3) first.
    Asserts #4-#5: the swapped joins are gone.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x10000000" readonly="true">
7ca418967c8419d67ca32b784e800020308400017c6301944e8000207c0802a6
900100049421fff04800001d308400077c63019480010014382100107c0803a6
4e800020388000037ca320961c8300037ca32b784e800020
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x10000000" name="wide_mul"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x10000010" name="add_one"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000001c" name="triple_plus"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x10000044" name="triple"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu wide_mul</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu add_one</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu triple_plus</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="bejoin #1: wide_mul returns the mulhw word (r3) as the high half" min="1" max="1">return CONCAT44\(\(int4\)\(\(uint8\)\(\(int8\)a1 \* a0\) >> 0x20\),a1 \* a0\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="bejoin #2: add_one carries into the r3 word, the high half" min="1" max="1">return CONCAT44\(a0 \+ \(uint4\)\(0xffffffff &lt;= a1\),a1 \+ 1\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="bejoin #3: the call output pair reads r3 as the high half" min="1" max="1">return CONCAT44\(\(int4\)\(\(uint8\)v1 >> 0x20\) \+ \(uint4\)\(0xfffffff9 &lt;= \(uint4\)v1\),\(uint4\)v1 \+ 7\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="bejoin #4: no join puts the low product first" min="0" max="0">CONCAT44\(a1 \* a0,</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="bejoin #5: no join puts the incremented low word first" min="0" max="0">CONCAT44\(a1 \+ 1,</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
